            <h2 class="display text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-center mb-10" data-i18n="faq_title">Frequently asked questions</h2>

            <details class="faq">
                <summary><span data-i18n="faq_q8">Which rate applies to me — buy or sell?</span></summary>
                <p data-i18n="faq_a8" data-i18n-html="">Rates are always quoted from the kantor's side — WE BUY or WE SELL the currency.<br><br><strong>We buy</strong> = you bring us currency. At a buy rate of EUR 4.40, for 100 euro you receive <strong>440 zł</strong>.<br><br><strong>We sell</strong> = you buy currency from us. At a sell rate of GBP 5.10, for 100 British pounds you pay <strong>510 zł</strong>.</p>
            </details>
            <details class="faq">
                <summary><span data-i18n="faq_q11">What is your spread?</span></summary>
                <p data-i18n="faq_a11" data-i18n-html="">A few groszy. At retail the difference between our buy and sell rate is at most 0.09 PLN (9 groszy). At wholesale it is 0.01–0.03 PLN*: that is the rate we show for <span class="wf">5 000 EUR</span>, and you can negotiate from the equivalent of as little as 500 EUR. Both tables, retail and wholesale, are always on our board and on this page. (*during Polish FX market hours)</p>
            </details>
            <details class="faq">
                <summary><span data-i18n="faq_q9">What is the difference between złoty (zł) and PLN?</span></summary>
                <p data-i18n="faq_a9">None — it is the same currency. PLN is the international code of the Polish złoty (like EUR for the euro), and zł is the everyday abbreviation you will see on price tags. 10 zł = 10 PLN.</p>
            </details>
            <details class="faq">
                <summary><span data-i18n="faq_q7">Which currencies do you exchange?</span></summary>
                <p data-i18n="faq_a7" data-i18n-html=""><span class="curr">🇪🇺 EUR (euro)</span><span class="curr">🇺🇸 USD (US dollars)</span><span class="curr">🇬🇧 GBP (British pounds)</span><span class="curr">🇨🇭 CHF (Swiss francs)</span><span class="curr">🇨🇦 CAD (Canadian dollars)</span><span class="curr">🇦🇺 AUD (Australian dollars)</span><span class="curr">🇩🇰 DKK (Danish krone)</span><span class="curr">🇸🇪 SEK (Swedish krona)</span><span class="curr">🇳🇴 NOK (Norwegian krone)</span><span class="curr">🇨🇿 CZK (Czech koruna)</span><span class="curr">🇭🇺 HUF (Hungarian forints)</span><span class="curr">🇯🇵 JPY (Japanese yen)</span><span class="curr">🇦🇪 AED (UAE dirhams)</span>— plus euro coins and withdrawn or damaged banknotes, after contacting us first. You can send photos on WhatsApp: <a href="https://wa.me/48889505057" target="_blank" rel="noopener">wa.me/48889505057</a></p>
            </details>
            <details class="faq">
                <summary><span data-i18n="faq_q2">Are the rates negotiable?</span></summary>
                <p data-i18n="faq_a2" data-i18n-html="">Yes. For larger amounts the rate is agreed individually at the counter. The wholesale rate can be negotiated from the equivalent of as little as 500 EUR; it depends on the amount, so just ask. The wholesale table on this page shows rates for <span class="wf">5 000 EUR</span> (spread 0.01–0.03 PLN*). (*during Polish FX market hours)</p>
            </details>
            <details class="faq">
                <summary><span data-i18n="faq_q3">Can I pay by card?</span></summary>
                <p data-i18n="faq_a3">We exchange cash only — like every traditional kantor in Poland.</p>
            </details>
            <details class="faq">
                <summary><span data-i18n="faq_q4">Do you buy old, withdrawn or damaged banknotes?</span></summary>
                <p data-i18n="faq_a4" data-i18n-html="">Yes — withdrawn series, damaged banknotes and euro coins. Call us first at <a href="tel:+48889505057">+48 889 505 057</a> or send photos on WhatsApp: <a href="https://wa.me/48889505057" target="_blank" rel="noopener">wa.me/48889505057</a></p>
            </details>
            <details class="faq">
                <summary><span data-i18n="faq_q5">Do I need an ID to exchange money?</span></summary>
                <p data-i18n="faq_a5">For everyday amounts — no. Polish anti-money-laundering law requires identification only for transactions from the equivalent of 15,000 EUR.</p>
            </details>
            <details class="faq">
                <summary><span data-i18n="faq_q6">Are you open on weekends?</span></summary>
                <p data-i18n="faq_a6" data-i18n-html="">Saturday: 10:00–20:30<br>Sunday: closed<br>Monday–Friday: 09:30–20:30</p>
            </details>
            <details class="faq">
                <summary><span data-i18n="faq_q10">Is Floriańska a safe street to exchange money?</span></summary>
                <p data-i18n="faq_a10">Honestly? The street's reputation is mixed — some offices in the area display only one attractive rate, which applies to the opposite direction of the exchange. Others display no rates at all, and some show rates for other currencies, such as ALL, RSD or JOD, labelled with € $ £ symbols to mislead you. That is exactly why we play with open cards: the full buy and sell table on display, 0% commission, and you can verify any exchange office — including ours — in 30 seconds via fresh Google Maps reviews. Honest exchange on Floriańska exists; just check the board and the reviews, and ask for the rate at the counter before you hand over cash.</p>
            </details>
            <details class="faq">
                <summary><span data-i18n="faq_q1">Is this the former "Kantor U Górali"?</span></summary>
                <p data-i18n="faq_a1">Yes — the same exchange office at Floriańska 37 and the same owner. We changed the name so it says out loud who we are: the black sheep among Kraków's exchange offices — the one that plays with open cards. Full buy and sell rates on display, 0% commission, no asterisks. The name changed; the rules didn't.</p>
            </details>
        </div>
